id: decisions/001-one-canon-per-concept
title: "One governing record per concept, lenses marked"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'canonical-multiplicity'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: canonical-multiplicity
axis_gloss: "whether one blessed artifact (schema, template, root, entry point, memory) governs, or several coexist in parallel"
poles:
  a: single-canonical-artifact
  b: plural-parallel-artifacts
split: genuinely-split
draft_status: full
leverage: 57
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(38) x split_weight(1) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.5)"

question: >
  When two records define the same thing (lacuna, the loop, the objective
  lifecycle, a promotion gate, a read order), does one of them govern, and does
  it say so?

why_it_matters: >
  core/orientation.yawn already says one model with non-canonical lenses, yet
  the corpus ships two lacuna definitions, a five-stage and a nine-stage
  objective lifecycle, and gate definitions that have drifted apart, so a reader
  cannot tell which binds. Once answered, a bot reading core/ inherits exactly
  one definition per concept, and a child Yawn that coins its own phrasing knows
  it must point at the canon rather than become a second one.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    14 judgments sit at single-canonical-artifact (10 authored) and 24 at
    plural-parallel-artifacts (6 authored). The repository asserts both directions
    on this axis.
  attributed_to: JUDGMENTS.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Which of the two lifecycles (five stages or nine) and which loop (six
    relations or eight stages) Dave regards as canon; and whether the
    friendly/technical name pairs and the two first_read_orders are lenses he
    wants kept or second canons he wants removed.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: Each concept has exactly one canonical record. A second record that
      restates it is merged into the first or removed; friendly names and alternate
      loop phrasings are dropped in favour of the technical one.
    cost: "Merging core/lacuna.yawn into core/target-and-lacuna.yawn, reconciling the two lifecycles, collapsing formal/human/north-star loop phrasings and the two first_read_orders; loses the friendly-name layer the repo uses for outsiders."
    risk: "Merges erase attribution and disagreement, which core/holarchy.yawn forbids ('Merge never erases source records or disagreement')."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ that, for a named list of concept keys (lacuna.kinds, objective lifecycle, movement loop, promotion gates, first_read_order), fails when more than one active record defines the key."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0029"
      - "J0051"
      - "J0074"
      - "J0093"
      - "J0099"
      - "J0114"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Parallel definitions may coexist. No record is required to name another
      as canonical; readers reconcile drift themselves.
    cost: "The gate definitions in the feedback loop keep drifting; the five- and nine-stage lifecycles stay unreconciled; one_model_rule in core/orientation.yawn is contradicted and must be softened."
    risk: "A bot that reads the five-stage lifecycle and a bot that reads the nine-stage one act on different contracts with nothing to detect it."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None can enforce this; the only record would be striking one_model_rule from core/orientation.yawn."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0047"
      - "J0049"
      - "J0067"
      - "J0115"
      - "J0241"
      - "J0253"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: Each concept has one canonical record. Any other record that restates or
      rephrases it must carry a typed pointer to the canon (same_as, derived_from,
      supersedes, or projection_of, as core/holarchy.yawn already defines) and must
      not contradict it. Friendly names and loop rephrasings are allowed only as
      marked lenses over the canon.
    cost: "Add pointers to core/lacuna.yawn or core/target-and-lacuna.yawn, core/motivation-and-purpose.yawn, automation/yawn.bot.feedback-loop.yawn and its sibling; decide which lifecycle and which loop are canon."
    risk: "Pointers get added mechanically without reconciling the content, so drift is now labelled but not fixed."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "A test in tests/ that, for the named concept list, finds exactly one record without a pointer and requires every other occurrence to carry same_as/derived_from/supersedes/projection_of to it; extend scripts/validate-hub-links.mjs to resolve those pointers."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0093"
      - "J0099"
      - "J0029"
      - "J0074"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Side A is where the authored rules live (10 authored of 14): one_model_rule,
    the folder-node rule, the canonical memory root. The six side-B judgments
    shown are all revealed, and four of them are plain drift (gates restated and
    already diverged, lacuna copied verbatim with no cross-reference, two
    lifecycles of different length). The repo already holds the C mechanism:
    core/orientation.yawn declares lenses non-canonical and core/holarchy.yawn
    defines supersedes and same_as, which J0241 notes are unused. So C writes down
    what the authored side already says while keeping the friendly-name and
    dual-read-order artifacts as marked lenses. Close on the lifecycle and loop:
    the inventory counts six authored judgments on side B beyond the sample, so
    some parallelism is intended.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "core/lacuna.yawn and core/target-and-lacuna.yawn: one carries supersedes or same_as to the other, or one is removed."
    - "core/objective-holon.yawn and core/motivation-and-purpose.yawn publish one lifecycle, or the shorter one carries derived_from and matches it stage for stage."
    - "A test in tests/ fails when a concept on the named list is defined in two active records with neither pointing at the other, and passes."
    - "The promotion-gate definitions in automation/yawn.bot.feedback-loop.yawn and its sibling match, or one references the other."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A new record restates lacuna.kinds or the objective lifecycle with no
    same_as/derived_from pointer and npm test stays green.
  status: proposed

resolves:
  - "J0029"
  - "J0051"
  - "J0074"
  - "J0093"
  - "J0099"
  - "J0114"
  - "J0047"
  - "J0049"
  - "J0067"
  - "J0115"
  - "J0241"
  - "J0253"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
